{"text": "\u2018Nicotine & Tobacco Research 2015, 1255-1282\nott osgneons\nOriginal investigation\n\u2018Advance Access publication January 7, 2015\n\nOriginal investigation\n\nThe Impact of Flavor Descriptors on\nNonsmoking Teens\u2019 and Adult Smokers\u2019 Interest\nin Electronic Cigarettes\n\nSaul Shiffman PhD\", Mark A. Sembower MS\", Jai\nKaren K. Gerlach PhD, MPH', Joseph G. Gitchell BA\u00ae\n\n\"Pinney Associates, Pittsburgh, PA; \"Department of Psychology, University of Pittsburgh, Pitsburgh, PA; *Pinney\n\u2018Associates, Bethesda, MD\n\nie L. Pillitteri PhD',\n\nCorresponding Author: Saul Shifan, PhD, Pinney Associates, 201 North Craig Steet, Suite 320, Pitsburg, PA 18213,\n\u2018USA Telophone: 412-567-178\n\nAbstract\nIntroduction: Smokers switching completely from combustible cigarettes to electronic cigarettes\n{e-cigarettes) are likoly to reduce health risk, suggesting that e-cigarettes should be made appeal-\ning to adult smokers. However, uptake of e-cigarettes by nonsmoking teens would add risk with-\n\u2018out benefit and should be avoided. Although e-cigarette flavors may appeal to adult smokers, the\n\u2018concern is that flavors might attract nonsmoking teens.\n\u2018Methods: Nonsmoking teens (n = 216, ages 13-17, no tobacco in past 6 months) and adult smok-\ners (n= 432, ages 19-80, smoking 3+ years; could have used e-cigarettes) were recruited from an\nInternet research panel. In assessments completed online (May 22, 2014 to June 13, 2014), par\nticipants indicated their interest (0-10 scale) in e-cigarettes paired with various flavor descriptors.\n\u2018These were mixed (order balanced) with similar flavor offerings for ice cream and bottled water\nto mask the focus on e-cigarettes and validate the assessment. Mixed models contrasted interest\nbetween teens and adults and among adults by e-cigaretts history.\nResults: Nonsmoking teons' interest in e-cigarettes was very low (mean = 0.41 \u00a30.14 [SE] on 0-10\nscale). Adult smokers\u2019 interest (1.730.10), while modest, was significantly higher overall (p <\n0001) and for each flavor (most p values < 0001). Teen interest did not vary by flavor (p =.75),\nbbut adult interest did (p <_.0001). Past-30-day adult e-cigarette users had the greatest interest in\ne-cigarettes, and their interest was most affected by flavor. Adults who never tried e-cigarettes had\n\u2018the lowest interest, yet still higher than nonsmoking teens\u2019 interest (p <.0001).\nConclusion: The e-cigarette flavors tested appealed more to adult smokers than to nonsmoking\n\u2018teens, but interest in flavors was low for both groups.\n\nIntroduction\n\u2018The effet of electronic cigarettes (-<garetes on public health s being\nhotly debated. Although nicotine itself is noe hares, iis known\nthat the harm of smoking overwhelmingly comes from the pods\n\u2018of tobacco combustion.\u2019 Accordingly it has been proposed that eciga-\nretes have the potential to dramatically decreas the rik to individuals\n\u2018ho completely switch to them from smoking combustible cigarettes\n\n{Kcurrent smokers switched to nieotine-only products sich a8 eign\nrete itis projected tha harm would be dramatically reduced.\n\u2018The public health debate, however, centers on the effets on the\n\u2018whole population, which isthe standard established i the law that\n\u2018gave the Food and Drug Administration regulatory authority over\ntobacco,\u201d and this depends heavily on who adopts e-<igaretes:\nUptake of e-cigaretes by individuals who would not otherwise\nsmoke does not decrease risk and could increase it. This could occur\n\n(\u00a9The Author 25. Pblishs by Ord Unies rss on baal tha Soci or Reszarch on aie and Tage. Al igh ese 1255\n\nFer sessions, lease ema uals peissonsGoup com\n\nS102 41 s9quandas vo see saguuw 022e901 PUP BUROOIN No AMIE nf Asia0S We Au-sNO|HO|O-/Ny OH PepROILAGR",
